[Chapter "Against ...e6"]
[Line "Nb5 and the knight on a6"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]

{Black's most common setup. White uses Nb5 to make Black spend a move covering c7.} 1. d4 {Start with the queen's pawn.} 1... d5 2. Nc3 {The Jobava idea: the knight comes to c3 before the c-pawn moves, ready for Nb5 or e4.} 2... Nf6 3. Bf4 {Develop the bishop outside the pawn chain before e3.} 3... e6 4. Nb5 {Attacks c7 straight away; Nxc7+ is a threat.} 4... Na6 {Covers c7, but the knight on a6 is clumsy.} 5. e3 {Solid support for d4; the f1 bishop can now develop.} 5... Be7 6. Nf3 {Develop and control e5.} 6... O-O 7. Be2 {Simple development. White castles next and can play c4 or Ne5 later.} *
